00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:08.420
NEHA PATTAN: Hello, everyone.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:10.540
Welcome to GCP Next 2018.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.690
I'm really glad that all
of you could make it.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.790
My name is Neha Pattan and I'm
a software engineer at Google.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:19.540
I work as a DO on VPC.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.110
I'm going to be joined by
Emanuele and Kamal today.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:25.540
And we are going to do a
deep dive into Google VPC.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.690
So to start off
with what is a VPC?

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:34.900
A VPC stands for Virtual
Private Cloud, a concept

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.510
that I'm sure most of
you are familiar with.

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.480
A virtual private cloud
is a logical isolation

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.610
of a section of your cloud.

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:45.580
And it is a cloud equivalent
of your traditional network

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:48.550
that you would run in
your own data center,

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.190
but with the extremely important
added benefits of running

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:54.400
on infrastructure that is
highly optimized for scale,

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.530
performance, and
reduced network costs.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:01.290
We have a 50-minute talk today.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.793
I'm going to be introducing
a few core VPC concepts

00:01:03.793 --> 00:01:05.459
and walking you through
some of the work

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:08.760
that we've been doing
over the past one year,

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:12.300
and also mentioning some best
practices that we recommend.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.330
Emanuele, who is a networking
product specialist at Google,

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:19.680
will be walking us through a
few example VPC topologies.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.454
And Kamal, who is our
guest from PayPal,

00:01:21.454 --> 00:01:23.370
will be walking us through
PayPal's experience

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:24.760
of using Google VPC.

00:01:27.391 --> 00:01:28.890
Now what is the
first thing that you

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:30.870
do when you want to check
whether your internet

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.862
connectivity is working
correctly or not?

00:01:33.862 --> 00:01:35.570
If you're a networking
expert, the answer

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.110
may be slightly different.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.810
But for most of the
world, the answer

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.450
is to open a web browser,
navigate to google.com,

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:46.796
and check whether that web
page loads correctly or not.

00:01:46.796 --> 00:01:49.340
Using Google Cloud gives
you the unique opportunity

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.090
of using the same underlying
network infrastructure that

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:56.150
powers all of Google's services,
like Search, Maps, YouTube,

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.090
Gmail.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:00.490
And VPC is a really simple
and intuitive interface

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.770
to this infrastructure.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:05.440
And it provides to you
programmable distributed

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:06.918
networking functions.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:12.940
So what's unique
about Google VPC?

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:16.660
Most importantly, it uses
the highly optimized, low

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:19.900
congestion and high bandwidth,
high quality of service

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.510
global network backbone.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:25.640
Now this is important
for many reasons.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.510
It allows you the capability
of creating virtual machines

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.910
anywhere in the
world in many regions

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:33.160
and having private
connectivity between all

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.900
of these virtual
machines by default,

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.510
so you don't need to set up
expensive VPN connections

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:39.890
or peering connections.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:43.610
You don't need to give your
virtual machines public IPs

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:45.140
in order for these
virtual machines

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:50.160
to be able to communicate with
each other across regions.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.280
It allows you to peer with
GCP at a single location

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:58.260
and to get access to all of
your workloads running globally.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:02:59.670
Using Google Cloud
Load Balancer,

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:02.394
you can create a global
load balancing application.

00:03:02.394 --> 00:03:04.560
And you can rest assured
that the load balancer will

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:11.090
send requests to the closest
backends that are healthy

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:14.010
and that are closest
to the end user that

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.835
is sending the request.

00:03:15.835 --> 00:03:17.460
Now this is really
important because it

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.500
improves the perception
of performance

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:23.010
of your application.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:26.310
Using a single global VPC
that is shareable in nature

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:29.090
allows you to administer
your network centrally,

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:31.830
while allowing thousands of
developers and development

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:34.560
teams in your
organization to create

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.570
their own projects,
to create and manage

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:39.620
their compute resources in.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.080
Through private access, you
can enable virtual machines

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:45.200
in your VPC to have
access to Google APIs,

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.650
like your GCS packets,
without having to assign

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:51.697
public IPs to these VMs.

00:03:51.697 --> 00:03:53.780
So the traffic that goes
from the virtual machines

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.400
to your storage
APIs, for example,

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.360
would use the network backbone
and would not use the internet.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:04.920
But all of these things
sound very 2017, right?

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.729
What is new about VPCs in 2018?

00:04:08.729 --> 00:04:10.770
We've been working really
hard over the past year

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:15.720
to build the model of VPCs
to be more secure, scalable,

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:19.230
performant, and easy to use.

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:22.980
All the data traffic in GCP
gets authenticated and encrypted

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.080
when it exits the
physical boundary that

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:30.280
is owned by Google or
operated on behalf of Google.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.610
We use the same DDoS
protection system in the cloud

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.400
as we do for the rest of
Google, so you get the benefit

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.300
of perimeter security.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:41.160
We built and announced a product
around this called Cloud Armor,

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:43.560
which allows you fine-grained
control over the rules

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:48.660
that you can specify for
the perimeter security.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:51.390
And these rules can be
IP blacklist, whitelists,

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:54.510
or WAF rules, or you can
specify geo-specific rules

00:04:54.510 --> 00:05:00.170
for allowing traffic into your
load balanced applications.

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:02.190
We provide network
layer security

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:04.740
through stateful connection
tracked firewall rules that

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.630
are implemented in a
distributed fashion

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.770
and so are very scalable.

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:16.360
We use Andromeda, which is
the SDN virtualization stack.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:20.070
And we have made several
improvements in the Andromeda

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:22.410
control plane infrastructure
over the past year, which

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.660
have enabled us to more
than double the number

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:27.255
of virtual machines that you
can create in a single VPC.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:33.290
We take performance
very seriously.

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:36.850
We launched Andromeda
2.1 late last year,

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:40.960
which gave a 40% reduction
in the cross-VM latencies

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:45.070
within a single zone
over Andromeda 2.0.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:46.870
And it does this
through an optimization

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:49.090
by bypassing the
hypervisor threads

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:51.790
for network traffic that is
going from the virtual machine

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:53.890
to the software switch
on the host machine.

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:58.430
We also made
several changes that

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:01.160
enable fine-grained
IAM access control

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.967
and integration with third party
services using VPC peering.

00:06:05.967 --> 00:06:07.675
Now, one of the more
exciting initiatives

00:06:07.675 --> 00:06:09.610
we have had within
the VPC team is

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:11.800
to take a
Kubernetes-first approach.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.770
And so we have made
several changes in VPC

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:17.860
inherently, in order to
better support Kubernetes

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.950
as a first-class
citizen through GKE.

00:06:24.066 --> 00:06:25.440
Now, whether you
are cloud-native

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:28.890
or you are also running your
work loads in your on-prem data

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.920
center or you may be running
workloads in another cloud

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:36.420
as well, you may start
small in a single region

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.790
or in a small set of regions.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:41.190
But as your application
grows and as you

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:45.030
need to expand globally, you
may expand to multiple regions.

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:48.240
But the configuration that
you did initially just

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.160
seamlessly works.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.490
And so the overhead of network
administration and management

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:55.500
doesn't grow over
the number of regions

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.830
that you need to put your
compute resources in.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:04.646
Now, let's walk through the
different aspects of VPC.

00:07:04.646 --> 00:07:06.770
The first aspect that I'm
going to be talking about

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:08.510
is that of a network.

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:11.210
Now, a network maps to
a global virtual network

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:14.450
that you can create
virtual machines in.

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:19.220
And it basically consists
of regional subnetworks that

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:23.270
have non-overlapping IP ranges.

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:25.530
Now before I explain the
concept of a Shared VPC,

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:29.000
I'm going to step back a bit
and talk to you about a project.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:30.810
Now, a project is
a granular unit

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:34.220
in GCP, which is a container
that you can put resources

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:35.834
inside.

00:07:35.834 --> 00:07:37.250
So if you want to
create resources

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:41.390
like firewall rules,
routing policies, a network,

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:43.970
or virtual machines,
or VPN connections,

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:47.490
you can create them
inside of a project.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.610
A project is also a
basic unit that you

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:51.920
can attach billing
accounts to, that you

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:54.680
can specify IAM policies
on, and that you

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:56.900
can manage [INAUDIBLE] on.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:00.470
So it gives you a container
that you can completely manage.

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:02.210
And then you can
have full control

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:06.910
over the lifecycle
management of your resources.

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:09.460
Now with Shared VPC,
you get the ability

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:12.100
to create a VPC centrally
in your organization

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.830
in a project called
the host project,

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:17.830
and to share that with
thousands of other projects

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:20.540
in your organization
called service projects,

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:23.050
that you can create virtual
machines in and have

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:26.774
these virtual machines be
connected to the Shared VPC.

00:08:26.774 --> 00:08:28.440
Now, this is really
important because it

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.840
allows you to scale your
organization to thousands

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:33.659
of developers or
development teams,

00:08:33.659 --> 00:08:35.580
while centrally
managing the firewall

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:38.072
rules, the routing policies,
the VPN connections,

00:08:38.072 --> 00:08:39.780
and not having to
create these every time

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:40.821
a new project is spun up.

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:47.200
So what's new in the space?

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.690
Now with the Shared
VPC, you can administer

00:08:49.690 --> 00:08:51.640
the Shared VPC in folders.

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:54.220
And so for example, if you
want to isolate your billing

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:56.980
pipeline VPC from
your web app VPC,

00:08:56.980 --> 00:09:01.030
then you can do so by having
separate folders for these two

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:04.330
projects and having separate
Shared VPC administrators who

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:07.780
can setup the
respective Shared VPCs.

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:10.030
One of the things that
we recommend doing here

00:09:10.030 --> 00:09:14.140
is to put all the service
projects for a given Shared VPC

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:16.900
set-up inside the same
folder so that you

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:22.149
can associate org level policies
on that folder at a later time.

00:09:22.149 --> 00:09:23.690
Another thing that
we recommend doing

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:27.140
is to use subnetwork
level permissions

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:31.280
so that you can specify
which service projects have

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:33.630
access to create virtual
machines in which specific

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:34.580
subnetworks.

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:38.259
That gives you fine-grained
access control.

00:09:38.259 --> 00:09:40.050
Another feature that
we have launched to GA

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:41.790
over the past year
is the ability

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:44.590
to create private
IP reservation.

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:48.260
And so you can reserve
RFC 1918 IP addresses.

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:50.895
And you can use these for
creating virtual machines

00:09:50.895 --> 00:09:53.700
or internal load balancers
in your service projects.

00:09:57.079 --> 00:09:58.620
Another feature that
we have launched

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:01.780
to GA over the past
year is alias IP ranges.

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:04.240
Now, alias IP ranges
give you the ability

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:07.300
to create alias or secondary
IPs and associate these

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:10.770
with network interfaces
of your virtual machines.

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:13.530
You can allocate alias IPs
through primary or secondary

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:17.480
ranges on your subnetwork.

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:20.900
Now if you use GKE to manage
workloads running in containers

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:23.930
on your GCE VMs, then alias
IP ranges just gives you

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:27.590
the ability to centrally
manage all of your RFC 1918 IP

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:28.880
addresses.

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:31.370
So your port IPs,
your node IPs, cluster

00:10:31.370 --> 00:10:35.060
IPs are allocated from the
primary and secondary ranges

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:37.880
of your subnetworks.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:41.210
It also gives native
support for routing,

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:44.600
and so it scales better and
you can create more nodes

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:47.340
in the cluster.

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:49.450
If you use GKE to
create an Ingress load

00:10:49.450 --> 00:10:52.770
balancer with a network endpoint
group, then you can rest

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:55.440
assured that the front
end of the load balancer

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:58.080
would load balance directly
to the pods that are back

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:01.084
ends of the load balancer
rather than having

00:11:01.084 --> 00:11:02.500
the front end of
the load balancer

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:04.590
load balance to
virtual machines,

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:07.890
which then have IP table
rules to route the packet

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:10.920
to the back end pods.

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:12.510
So using network
endpoint groups,

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:17.880
we get rid of this extra
hop in the request path.

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:21.000
And so it is basically
optimizing the load balancing

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:24.280
application performance.

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:27.330
It also gives native
support for health checking.

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:30.240
And so we are able to health
check the pods directly rather

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:32.130
than health check
the virtual machines.

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:38.640
So as I mentioned
before, alias IPs

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:40.830
can be allocated from
primary or secondary ranges

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:43.334
in a subnetwork.

00:11:43.334 --> 00:11:44.750
If you're running
out of IP space,

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:47.930
you can add more secondary
ranges on the subnetwork

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:51.244
or you can expand the existing
primary or second ranges.

00:11:51.244 --> 00:11:52.910
Now, one of the things
that we recommend

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:55.070
doing when you are
planning your IP space

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:56.570
is to plan it in
such a way that you

00:11:56.570 --> 00:12:00.042
leave enough room for growth.

00:12:00.042 --> 00:12:02.000
Now if you're running an
application on your VM

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:06.170
and you're using an alias
IP to run the application,

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:10.490
then you may want to move
the IP from one VM to another

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:13.280
if the pod that you're running
the application in dies

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:15.650
or the application crashes.

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:19.340
And you can do this by
deleting the alias IP on VM1,

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:22.550
adding it to VM2, creating
a pod there, and starting

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:24.380
your application in VM2.

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:26.690
So this is basically
how you migrate an IP

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:28.280
from one VM to another VM.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:35.280
Now, the next aspect I will
be talking about is security.

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.800
As I mentioned before, we
provide network layer firewall

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:39.000
rules.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:40.890
But the way we implement
these firewall rules

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:45.130
is not through a
metal box or a proxy,

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:47.930
and so there are no
single choke points.

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:51.560
Firewall rules are programmed
on every single host machine

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:55.700
that has virtual machines
running in your VPC

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:59.040
and so they are truly
distributed in nature.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:01.140
The rules themselves
are flexible in allowing

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:06.000
ingress, egress, allow or
deny rules using priorities.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:09.120
You can use tags to easily group
the virtual machines that you

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:13.128
want to apply specific
firewall policies on.

00:13:13.128 --> 00:13:17.040
Now, when a developer adds a
tag to their virtual machine,

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:20.270
there is no [? ACL ?] check
that happens by default.

00:13:20.270 --> 00:13:22.270
This is because if you
own a virtual machine,

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:24.300
if you have access
to set tags on it,

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:26.220
then you are able
to associate any tag

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:28.109
with the virtual machine.

00:13:28.109 --> 00:13:30.150
Now, if the service provided
by your organization

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:33.150
is security-sensitive,
internally or externally,

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:36.320
then this may be problematic.

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:38.540
And we solve this
problem using firewalls

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:40.530
with service accounts.

00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:43.810
So we are happy to announce that
this is now available in GA.

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:45.560
You can associate
a service account

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:47.600
and you can create
that as a source

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:49.880
or a target of a firewall.

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:52.930
And you can rest assured that
when a developer associates

00:13:52.930 --> 00:13:55.010
a service account
with their VM, then

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:57.390
there is an [? ACL ?]
check that happens.

00:13:57.390 --> 00:14:00.600
They need to have IAM permission
on using that service account.

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:08.280
Now, you configured
your VPC topology,

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:11.910
you carved out the IP space,
you created the subnetworks

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:14.820
in different regions, and you
created the firewall rules

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:19.064
to ensure that the traffic
within the VPC is secure.

00:14:19.064 --> 00:14:20.730
Now, the next thing
you would like to do

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:23.940
is connect your VPC
running in the cloud

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:26.095
with your on-prem data center.

00:14:26.095 --> 00:14:29.262
Now, there are multiple
ways of doing this.

00:14:29.262 --> 00:14:31.220
If bandwidth is not of
a major concern for you,

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:36.350
if you are OK with the
overhead of IPsec encryption--

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:38.450
it basically uses the internet--

00:14:38.450 --> 00:14:41.750
then you can use VPN.

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:45.320
And there are two ways of
using VPN connections as well.

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:47.120
You can either
create static routes

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:49.910
to your VPN tunnel,
which basically

00:14:49.910 --> 00:14:52.190
allow you to statically
specify the prefixes that

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:54.860
should be routed to
your on-prem or you

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:58.190
can use Cloud Router,
which runs as a BGP daemon,

00:14:58.190 --> 00:15:02.449
to exchange routes with
your on-prem dynamically.

00:15:02.449 --> 00:15:04.490
Now one thing worth
mentioning about Cloud Router

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:07.160
is that unlike what
the name might suggest,

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:08.975
it's not a traditional router.

00:15:08.975 --> 00:15:10.850
And so packets that are
going to your on-prem

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:13.160
or coming from your
on-prem to the VPC

00:15:13.160 --> 00:15:16.430
are not going through
the Cloud Router.

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:18.710
Cloud Router is a
control plane component.

00:15:18.710 --> 00:15:22.130
And it basically is responsible
for programming the routes

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:25.610
that it learns from the on-prem
onto the host machines that

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:30.050
have virtual machines in your
VPC running on those host

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:32.442
machines.

00:15:32.442 --> 00:15:34.400
Now if bandwidth is of
a major concern for you,

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:37.040
then you can use Interconnects.

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:40.100
If you would like to
directly peer with Google,

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:43.880
if you have a public ESN and
you want to basically connect

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:46.610
with us at one of our POPs,
or Points Of Presence,

00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:49.920
then you can use
Dedicated Interconnect.

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:52.746
If you are doing the same
thing using a carrier's

00:15:52.746 --> 00:15:54.120
infrastructure
and you are paying

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.100
the carrier for the
service, then you

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:59.240
would use Partner Interconnect.

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:01.880
We're really excited that
both Dedicated and Partner

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:05.940
Interconnect are now
generally available.

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:08.419
There's a talk at 11:40
on hybrid connectivity

00:16:08.419 --> 00:16:10.210
that I would highly
recommend checking out.

00:16:12.527 --> 00:16:14.110
Now, a couple of
features that I would

00:16:14.110 --> 00:16:18.430
like to talk about that are new
in the dynamic routing space

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:23.580
are global BGP routing and
custom route advertisements.

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:25.540
Using global BGP
routing, you can

00:16:25.540 --> 00:16:28.600
announce prefixes that
belong to subnetworks

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:32.850
in other regions using a
Cloud Router in one region.

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:35.890
And we recommend doing this
for enabling redundancy

00:16:35.890 --> 00:16:39.840
in your connection
to your on-prem.

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:42.760
And so you can have Cloud
Routers in two regions

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:44.800
to your global VPC.

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:48.491
And you can enable global
BGP routing on your VPC.

00:16:48.491 --> 00:16:50.740
And this would enable you
to have local routes as well

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:53.510
as remote routes through
the other region.

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:58.280
Now, Cloud Router will add
weight to the remote routes,

00:16:58.280 --> 00:17:01.100
so it will always
prefer the local routes.

00:17:01.100 --> 00:17:04.130
But if your local connection--
if your local VPN connection

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:07.730
or Interconnect-- goes
down, then the remote route

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:08.750
would be used.

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:11.720
And so you can fall
back to using the routes

00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:14.220
in a different region.

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:18.556
So this gives you
higher availability.

00:17:18.556 --> 00:17:20.180
You can use custom
route advertisements

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:25.970
to choose prefixes that you want
to announce to your on-prem.

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:27.740
And so you can choose
prefixes that you

00:17:27.740 --> 00:17:29.850
don't want to announce.

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:32.250
You can use custom route
advertisements also

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:33.930
to announce prefixes
that don't belong

00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:36.750
to subnetworks in your VPC.

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:41.310
And these may be RFC 1918 IB
or IP ranges, or non-RFC 1918

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:41.810
as well.

00:17:44.459 --> 00:17:46.250
Now, the next thing
I'm going to talk about

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:49.430
is using third party services.

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:51.560
Now, Shared VPC was
designed with the intention

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:55.970
of having private connectivity
between virtual machines

00:17:55.970 --> 00:17:58.150
in one organization.

00:17:58.150 --> 00:17:59.970
But if you want to
connect two VPCs in two

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:01.710
different organizations--

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:04.710
the typical consumer
and producer scenario--

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:08.120
then you can use VPC peering.

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:12.200
VPC peering is also not
implemented through a proxy

00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:15.880
and it is truly
distributed in nature.

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:18.020
And so there is no
single point of failure

00:18:18.020 --> 00:18:20.570
with VPC peering either.

00:18:20.570 --> 00:18:23.840
When you peer to VPCs,
you get full connection

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:26.510
and you get full connectivity
to all the virtual machines

00:18:26.510 --> 00:18:28.790
in the peer VPC.

00:18:28.790 --> 00:18:31.100
And you also get access
to all the services

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:33.740
that the peer VPC is exposing
through internal load

00:18:33.740 --> 00:18:36.275
balancers.

00:18:36.275 --> 00:18:37.650
Now one of the
things that I want

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:41.190
to mention about both Shared
VPC as well as VPC peering

00:18:41.190 --> 00:18:46.260
is that in both cases, you get
high throughput and bandwidth.

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:48.240
And you get the same
throughput and bandwidth

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:50.430
as though if you were
to create those two

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:55.020
virtual machines in the same
network in the same project.

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:58.530
And this is because that
aspect of network topology

00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:00.690
is pretty much transparent
to the SDN data plane.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:06.560
If you're a service
provider and you

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:09.110
would like to have a
management interface

00:19:09.110 --> 00:19:11.210
to the virtual machines
that are serving traffic

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:14.480
to your consumer,
then you can do so

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:17.270
by creating a virtual
machine with multiple network

00:19:17.270 --> 00:19:19.460
interfaces.

00:19:19.460 --> 00:19:21.410
You can create up to
eight network interfaces

00:19:21.410 --> 00:19:24.350
on your virtual
machine and the traffic

00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:26.060
basically on those
two network interfaces

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:28.080
will be completely isolated.

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:30.262
So your data plane network
will be fully isolated

00:19:30.262 --> 00:19:31.470
from your management network.

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:36.930
Now, the last aspect
I'll be talking about

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:39.380
is that of monitoring.

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:42.800
As you know, GCE is
integrated with Stackdriver.

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:45.500
And so if you use features
like global load balancing

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:47.420
or internal load
balancer, then you'll

00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:51.690
be able to view the logs for
these features in Stackdriver.

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:53.240
We're really excited
to now announce

00:19:53.240 --> 00:19:57.040
the general availability
of VPC Flow Logs.

00:19:57.040 --> 00:20:00.620
VPC Flow Logs allow
you to view the VPC

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:06.020
flows, which are aggregated
over five seconds by connection.

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:10.780
And you can enable this feature
on a per subnetwork basis.

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:14.500
On the console, you can choose
the log type as VPC Flows

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:18.640
and you can then view the
connections that are logged.

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:20.700
Each log will show
you the [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:22.780
of the connection
along with other meta

00:20:22.780 --> 00:20:25.450
information like source
dest VPC, source dest

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:29.950
subnetwork, and other
annotation information like geo

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:34.520
annotations that we add on
top of that on the logs.

00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:37.726
You can export this to
BigQuery or Pub/Sub.

00:20:37.726 --> 00:20:39.850
And you can use this feature
for network forensics,

00:20:39.850 --> 00:20:41.680
for troubleshooting
your network,

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:44.170
as well as for analyzing
the different traffic

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:47.410
patterns in your network
across geographies,

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:49.720
and to use that information
to better improve

00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:51.880
the topology of your
network to reduce costs.

00:20:56.150 --> 00:20:58.710
Now, you may have started small
in a single or a small set

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:00.770
of regions, but the
configuration that you

00:21:00.770 --> 00:21:02.870
did initially by
creating the network,

00:21:02.870 --> 00:21:06.500
by creating the firewall rules,
by setting up VPC peering--

00:21:06.500 --> 00:21:08.750
these things just
seamlessly work

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:12.940
as you grow to more regions.

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:16.720
With the VPC design, we make
sure that the cost and overhead

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:19.210
of network administration
and management

00:21:19.210 --> 00:21:22.720
doesn't grow linearly with
the scale of your network

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:23.591
footprint.

00:21:26.420 --> 00:21:28.160
We really think of
this model of VPCs

00:21:28.160 --> 00:21:30.230
as being the true
next generation of how

00:21:30.230 --> 00:21:32.300
VPCs are defined in the cloud.

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:35.660
And we're really excited
to see how you use them.

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:37.970
We've been working
closely with some

00:21:37.970 --> 00:21:43.880
of the top names in education,
banking, software, technology,

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:45.920
mobile labs, you
know, you name it.

00:21:45.920 --> 00:21:48.140
And they've been giving
us some excellent feedback

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:50.300
on how to improve our features.

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:54.480
You know, it's really
been a fun ride so far.

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:57.010
Now, three things that
I want you to take away

00:21:57.010 --> 00:22:02.236
from this presentation are
that VPCs are global in nature.

00:22:02.236 --> 00:22:04.360
They use the same underlying
network infrastructure

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:06.550
as the rest of Google
that allows you

00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:09.562
to do amazing things at scale.

00:22:09.562 --> 00:22:11.200
They are highly performant.

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:12.990
We take performance
very seriously.

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:16.740
As I mentioned earlier, we have
been making a lot of progress

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:19.200
in improving performance
of our network,

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:21.730
but we're also looking for
new ways of doing this.

00:22:21.730 --> 00:22:23.410
We have a lot of
ongoing investment,

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:27.000
and so you can expect to see
more performance improvements

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:30.110
in the coming future.

00:22:30.110 --> 00:22:33.550
And lastly, that
VPCs are easy to use.

00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:37.210
As you can imagine, the network
is very powerful, but also very

00:22:37.210 --> 00:22:38.200
complex.

00:22:38.200 --> 00:22:40.600
And VPCs provide a simple
and intuitive interface

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:44.950
to this complex infrastructure
that allows you extensibility,

00:22:44.950 --> 00:22:50.049
access control, and integration
with third party services.

00:22:50.049 --> 00:22:51.590
And with that, I
would like to invite

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:55.122
Emanuele onstage to share a
few example VPC topologies.

00:22:55.122 --> 00:22:56.080
EMANUELE MAZZA: Thanks.

00:22:56.080 --> 00:22:58.190
NEHA PATTAN: Thanks.

00:22:58.190 --> 00:23:00.050
EMANUELE MAZZA: Thanks, Neha.

00:23:00.050 --> 00:23:02.400
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:04.760 --> 00:23:07.430
So we're going to run some
through example topologies

00:23:07.430 --> 00:23:11.780
that uses all the concept
and that Neha just explained.

00:23:11.780 --> 00:23:16.010
Before going over that, I
just want to quickly recap

00:23:16.010 --> 00:23:17.700
a couple of things.

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:20.000
So if you look at from
the routing point of view,

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:22.220
a VPC is really
a routing domain.

00:23:22.220 --> 00:23:24.350
That's what you get
when you create a VPC.

00:23:24.350 --> 00:23:27.500
It does provide you
internal IP connectivity

00:23:27.500 --> 00:23:29.330
across all your subnetworks--

00:23:29.330 --> 00:23:31.970
doesn't matter in which
region they exist--

00:23:31.970 --> 00:23:36.320
and without actually the traffic
leaving the Google network.

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:39.770
So all the traffic stays
inside the Google network.

00:23:39.770 --> 00:23:42.110
Neha mentioned that we
have a project concept.

00:23:42.110 --> 00:23:43.840
So the project is
a unit of building.

00:23:43.840 --> 00:23:45.140
It's a unit of permissions.

00:23:45.140 --> 00:23:46.640
It's a unit of resources.

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:49.490
And yes, you can have multiple
VPCs in the same project.

00:23:49.490 --> 00:23:54.500
If you do that, you get two
completely disjoint routing

00:23:54.500 --> 00:23:57.950
domains, which means,
like in the example here,

00:23:57.950 --> 00:24:01.280
you can have two VPCs with
overlapping IP subnets.

00:24:01.280 --> 00:24:05.000
So in this picture, you
can see that VPC1 and VPC2

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:07.760
as overlapping in us-west2.

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:10.770
Of course, this is possible
because they are disjoint

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:14.510
and they do not speak
across themself by design

00:24:14.510 --> 00:24:17.570
unless you want it to.

00:24:17.570 --> 00:24:19.850
And then the Cloud Router.

00:24:19.850 --> 00:24:22.720
So as Neha was saying, it's
the EBGP speaker process.

00:24:22.720 --> 00:24:28.000
Think about it like a route
processor in a physical router

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:28.890
world.

00:24:28.890 --> 00:24:31.180
It does not do data plane.

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:33.460
The whole goal in
life of this guy

00:24:33.460 --> 00:24:37.842
is to get routes from outside
and program them into the VPC,

00:24:37.842 --> 00:24:42.916
and gets route from the
VPC and send them outside.

00:24:42.916 --> 00:24:44.290
As you can see in
the picture, it

00:24:44.290 --> 00:24:47.210
can programs route
across all regions.

00:24:47.210 --> 00:24:50.740
So in this example, the Cloud
Router exists in us-west1,

00:24:50.740 --> 00:24:54.010
but its able also to advertise
subnetwork that exists

00:24:54.010 --> 00:24:59.380
in us-west2, which
is the 10.240.1/24.

00:24:59.380 --> 00:25:01.810
And they also are able to
advertise subnetworks that do

00:25:01.810 --> 00:25:09.930
not exist in your VPC-- in
this example, the 10.239.2/24.

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:15.810
OK, now that these two
concepts are clear, let's

00:25:15.810 --> 00:25:19.350
start looking at some
potential topologies.

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:25.500
So in this one, we're using VPC
peering in a producer consumer

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:26.580
scenario.

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:30.864
So we have on top, a provider
project in the blue org.

00:25:30.864 --> 00:25:33.030
And at the bottom, we've
got two consumer projects--

00:25:33.030 --> 00:25:35.580
one in the green org
and one in the red org.

00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:37.800
So please know that these
three projects are in three

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:39.875
different organizations.

00:25:39.875 --> 00:25:42.000
And now, what the provider
projects want to provide

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:46.922
is common services that must
be used from both consumer

00:25:46.922 --> 00:25:48.630
project in the green
org and the consumer

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:50.320
project in the red org.

00:25:50.320 --> 00:25:52.700
So the way I can do this, I
do this through VPC peering.

00:25:52.700 --> 00:25:55.710
So I do VPC peering
from the green org VPC

00:25:55.710 --> 00:26:00.660
to the leftmost VPC in
the provider project.

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:04.110
And the red one is peering
with the rightmost one.

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:06.760
Note that here I got
full overlapping IP

00:26:06.760 --> 00:26:08.190
across the consumer projects.

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:10.930
Both consumer project are
using the same subnetworks.

00:26:10.930 --> 00:26:15.860
That's why I need two different
VPCs in the provider project.

00:26:15.860 --> 00:26:18.090
Tenant isolation in
the provider project

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:21.360
is done by default
because those two VPCs are

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:23.130
separated routing
domains, so there

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:25.270
is no connectivity possible.

00:26:25.270 --> 00:26:28.600
You don't need to use
firewall rules for that.

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:32.300
Shared VPC-- so I'm
going to show you

00:26:32.300 --> 00:26:33.770
a simple example
of how shared VPC

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:36.950
can be used with a
multiple tier application.

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:40.880
And the goal of this topology
is to basically centralize

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:43.640
networking and security
configurations and operations

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:45.700
in the host project.

00:26:45.700 --> 00:26:50.860
And by using shared VPC
and giving different users

00:26:50.860 --> 00:26:53.560
different permissions to
use different subnets--

00:26:53.560 --> 00:26:55.190
and we'll see that--

00:26:55.190 --> 00:26:59.320
and then I will be able
to have each team--

00:26:59.320 --> 00:27:01.420
the team of the front
end service project

00:27:01.420 --> 00:27:03.120
and the back end
service project--

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:07.580
manage their own
compute by themselves.

00:27:07.580 --> 00:27:09.250
And this is, of course,
fully compatible

00:27:09.250 --> 00:27:11.200
with load balancers
and flow logs.

00:27:11.200 --> 00:27:13.130
So how does that work?

00:27:13.130 --> 00:27:15.100
So basically, first,
in this project,

00:27:15.100 --> 00:27:18.130
I create a subnetwork--
an orange subnetwork.

00:27:18.130 --> 00:27:21.520
And it's connected to
the shared VPC router.

00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:24.340
And I give permission
to the orange user

00:27:24.340 --> 00:27:26.030
to use the subnetwork.

00:27:26.030 --> 00:27:28.960
Subnetwork is an object,
so you can give permission

00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:30.670
to this object.

00:27:30.670 --> 00:27:33.370
Now what happens
is this orange user

00:27:33.370 --> 00:27:36.610
that exists in the service
project logs into the service

00:27:36.610 --> 00:27:39.190
project, create
instances, and he

00:27:39.190 --> 00:27:42.840
sees that subnetwork as
available to connect instances

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:47.740
onto, even if that subnetwork
does exist in the host project.

00:27:47.740 --> 00:27:50.200
From here on, from the
networking point of view,

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:52.600
those two instances is
just like if they are

00:27:52.600 --> 00:27:54.550
connected to the host project.

00:27:54.550 --> 00:27:56.270
From the compute
point of view, they

00:27:56.270 --> 00:27:58.060
exist in the service project.

00:27:58.060 --> 00:28:01.270
So building for the instances
is on the service project.

00:28:01.270 --> 00:28:05.500
Now from there, the orange user
create a global load balancer

00:28:05.500 --> 00:28:08.140
protecting the front end with
Cloud Armor, for example,

00:28:08.140 --> 00:28:11.370
and [INAUDIBLE] traffic
from the internet.

00:28:11.370 --> 00:28:15.430
Now, I'm also creating another
subnetwork in the host project.

00:28:15.430 --> 00:28:17.740
And this time, I'm
giving it permission

00:28:17.740 --> 00:28:20.560
to use this subnetwork
to the blue user.

00:28:20.560 --> 00:28:23.661
The blue user is a user that
exists in the back end service

00:28:23.661 --> 00:28:24.160
project.

00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:28.630
So what this user does is
logs into his service project

00:28:28.630 --> 00:28:32.080
and create an ILB,
create instances.

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:34.870
And as the orange
user previously, he

00:28:34.870 --> 00:28:37.300
sees the subnetwork
in the host project

00:28:37.300 --> 00:28:40.690
as available to connect
his instances to

00:28:40.690 --> 00:28:44.440
and available to create the VIP
of an internal load balancer.

00:28:44.440 --> 00:28:46.850
And from here, I got
full IP connectivity

00:28:46.850 --> 00:28:49.270
between front end
and back end, exactly

00:28:49.270 --> 00:28:52.690
like those two set
of instances are

00:28:52.690 --> 00:28:58.980
connected to the same
VPC on the host project.

00:28:58.980 --> 00:29:02.820
Same idea, but on a
different concept.

00:29:02.820 --> 00:29:08.100
So I want to use a network
virtual appliance to provide

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:12.030
security and networking
services to multiple projects

00:29:12.030 --> 00:29:14.020
at the same time.

00:29:14.020 --> 00:29:17.490
So what I can do is create
a network virtual appliance

00:29:17.490 --> 00:29:19.230
which is a multinic appliance.

00:29:19.230 --> 00:29:23.490
And as Neha was saying,
a multinic instance--

00:29:23.490 --> 00:29:26.050
appliance, in this case--

00:29:26.050 --> 00:29:27.970
in your multinic's
unique instance,

00:29:27.970 --> 00:29:32.470
each interface needs to be
connected to a different VPC.

00:29:32.470 --> 00:29:35.820
So here in this example,
I got those two VPCs.

00:29:35.820 --> 00:29:38.220
Let's say they are
two internal VPCs.

00:29:38.220 --> 00:29:41.580
And I'm using the same
concept of shared VPCs,

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:44.390
so I'm sharing [INAUDIBLE]
with the orange user

00:29:44.390 --> 00:29:45.627
and with the blue users.

00:29:45.627 --> 00:29:48.210
And they create their instances
and the internal load balancer

00:29:48.210 --> 00:29:50.670
exactly like before.

00:29:50.670 --> 00:29:52.850
And then the network
virtual appliance also has

00:29:52.850 --> 00:29:57.590
a northbound-- well, I should
say eastbound in this picture--

00:29:57.590 --> 00:30:02.010
external interface that is
connected to the external VPC--

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:03.780
so the one on the right--

00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:07.290
that does BGP with the Cloud
Router with your on-prem.

00:30:07.290 --> 00:30:10.410
So in this example,
all the instances--

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:13.200
VM1, VM2, VM3, and VM4--

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:16.650
do get connectivity with on-prem
and do leverage networking

00:30:16.650 --> 00:30:19.150
and security services provided
centrally by the network

00:30:19.150 --> 00:30:21.537
virtual appliance
in the host project.

00:30:24.810 --> 00:30:30.210
The next one is a more
complex one, I would say,

00:30:30.210 --> 00:30:32.160
but not that much--

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:35.100
Shared VPC with
shared Interconnect.

00:30:35.100 --> 00:30:37.120
So what's the goal?

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:39.910
The goal is that I want to
leverage the same Dedicated

00:30:39.910 --> 00:30:42.630
Interconnect across
multiple projects.

00:30:42.630 --> 00:30:43.350
Why?

00:30:43.350 --> 00:30:46.530
Because Dedicated Interconnect
is a precious resource.

00:30:46.530 --> 00:30:50.050
It provides [INAUDIBLE]
connectivity and it has a cost.

00:30:50.050 --> 00:30:54.310
So I want to use it
as much as possible.

00:30:54.310 --> 00:30:56.730
And I want to keep the
operational and management

00:30:56.730 --> 00:31:00.810
advantages of the Shared VPC
model that I showed before.

00:31:00.810 --> 00:31:04.410
And also I want to allocate
cost across projects

00:31:04.410 --> 00:31:06.960
in a meaningful way.

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:08.940
So how can we meet these goals?

00:31:08.940 --> 00:31:13.180
So first, so we create one
project where the Dedicated

00:31:13.180 --> 00:31:15.020
Interconnect exists--

00:31:15.020 --> 00:31:16.410
is created.

00:31:16.410 --> 00:31:20.280
And then you have one or
more shared VPC host project,

00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:22.230
each one with its
own Cloud Router

00:31:22.230 --> 00:31:24.940
and each one with its own
Interconnect attachment.

00:31:24.940 --> 00:31:27.600
As we will see, those
Interconnect attachment

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:29.730
will use Dedicated
Interconnect that

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:31.470
lives in a different project.

00:31:31.470 --> 00:31:36.940
Because again, everything
in GCP, it's an object.

00:31:36.940 --> 00:31:40.310
So also the Dedicated
Interconnect is an object

00:31:40.310 --> 00:31:42.480
and has permissions.

00:31:42.480 --> 00:31:43.900
How you do security?

00:31:43.900 --> 00:31:45.900
The best practice
Neha recommended

00:31:45.900 --> 00:31:49.100
is to use firewall rules
based on service account.

00:31:49.100 --> 00:31:54.100
And VPC Flow Logs will give you
complete visibility of traffic,

00:31:54.100 --> 00:31:58.590
including the service
VPC and the host VPC.

00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:03.750
So visually, how
that looks like?

00:32:03.750 --> 00:32:09.830
So if you look here first, so
this is the physical world.

00:32:09.830 --> 00:32:14.180
So these are [INAUDIBLE] sites
and you have your router here.

00:32:14.180 --> 00:32:16.950
And this is our
peering fabric device.

00:32:16.950 --> 00:32:21.160
So this is a site where we
provide dedicated interconnect.

00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:23.500
And this DI is the
Dedicated Interconnect.

00:32:23.500 --> 00:32:25.300
Physically it's a port
channel, so that's

00:32:25.300 --> 00:32:27.430
where the connection is built.

00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:30.210
Now if you forget
about the physical

00:32:30.210 --> 00:32:33.450
and think about the
cloud as an object model,

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:36.799
the Dedicated Interconnect is an
object that lives in a project.

00:32:36.799 --> 00:32:38.340
So you go into a
project and you say,

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:41.250
I want a Dedicated Interconnect.

00:32:41.250 --> 00:32:46.700
Now, we got in this
example two shared VPC.

00:32:46.700 --> 00:32:48.760
And the structure is
the same as the one

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:51.490
that I was basically
showing you before.

00:32:51.490 --> 00:32:57.130
And what is to be noticed here,
that each of those two shared

00:32:57.130 --> 00:32:58.780
VPC-- the host project--

00:32:58.780 --> 00:33:02.860
has a VLAN attachment
and a Cloud Router.

00:33:02.860 --> 00:33:06.550
So note that VLAN
attachment is an object that

00:33:06.550 --> 00:33:08.860
lives in the host
project, but it

00:33:08.860 --> 00:33:12.490
is using Dedicated Interconnect
that lives in the Dedicated

00:33:12.490 --> 00:33:14.000
Interconnect project.

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:16.150
So you can have a single
project with the Dedicated

00:33:16.150 --> 00:33:16.720
Interconnect.

00:33:16.720 --> 00:33:18.845
And you can use it to
basically share the bandwidth

00:33:18.845 --> 00:33:22.740
across multiple, in this
case, host projects,

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:25.210
of a shared VPC configuration.

00:33:25.210 --> 00:33:29.260
Of course, if you want, you
can have full overlapping IP

00:33:29.260 --> 00:33:33.550
support because, from
our side, the tenancy's

00:33:33.550 --> 00:33:36.490
provided by the fact that
we have two different VPCs.

00:33:36.490 --> 00:33:38.620
And on your side and the
customer side, of course,

00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:40.090
you have to terminate
[INAUDIBLE] that

00:33:40.090 --> 00:33:40.780
is overlapping.

00:33:40.780 --> 00:33:43.510
You have to terminate them into
different VRF, for example,

00:33:43.510 --> 00:33:47.920
and then bring the tenancy
inside your network to MPLS VPN

00:33:47.920 --> 00:33:50.470
or whatever you are use to.

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:58.140
So we basically met
all these goals.

00:33:58.140 --> 00:34:02.100
And a couple of consideration--
so how about the billing?

00:34:02.100 --> 00:34:03.750
So the Dedicated
Interconnect gets

00:34:03.750 --> 00:34:06.910
billed against that project,
the project where it exists.

00:34:06.910 --> 00:34:11.310
The VLAN attachment gets billed
against the host project.

00:34:11.310 --> 00:34:13.620
The Egress traffic
from the instances

00:34:13.620 --> 00:34:17.280
gets billed on the
service project

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:20.316
where those instances lives,
not on the host project.

00:34:20.316 --> 00:34:22.440
So if you have multiple
teams, different teams that

00:34:22.440 --> 00:34:27.030
manage the service
project, you can

00:34:27.030 --> 00:34:32.159
know how much traffic and Egress
cost they are basically using.

00:34:32.159 --> 00:34:34.650
There's a common DNS space
spanning each Shared VPC

00:34:34.650 --> 00:34:35.150
domain.

00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:39.050
This is a native
capability of Shared VPC.

00:34:39.050 --> 00:34:41.210
And VPC Flow Logs, I
was saying, contains

00:34:41.210 --> 00:34:44.420
information, both about the
service project and the host

00:34:44.420 --> 00:34:44.940
projects.

00:34:44.940 --> 00:34:48.860
So if you look at the flow
logs, in the source instance

00:34:48.860 --> 00:34:51.170
and destination
instance field, you

00:34:51.170 --> 00:34:54.560
will see the service project
where the instances actually

00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:55.639
leaves.

00:34:55.639 --> 00:34:59.970
And if you look in source
VPC and destination VPC,

00:34:59.970 --> 00:35:03.825
you will see the
host project to which

00:35:03.825 --> 00:35:04.950
the instances are attached.

00:35:07.900 --> 00:35:10.050
So I have two more.

00:35:10.050 --> 00:35:13.380
One is a classical high
throughput VPN connection.

00:35:13.380 --> 00:35:20.280
So in this example, you have
two VPN gateways in your VPC.

00:35:20.280 --> 00:35:23.340
In this example, they are
in two different regions.

00:35:23.340 --> 00:35:27.240
And you have multiple VPN
tunnels for each VPN gateway.

00:35:29.850 --> 00:35:33.780
Since we can do equal cost
multipath per flow, of course,

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:36.570
from GCP to on-prem, this
basically gives you a high

00:35:36.570 --> 00:35:40.410
throughput connectivity
between your on-prem--

00:35:40.410 --> 00:35:45.000
sorry, your GCP project
and the on-prem.

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:49.120
There are multiple ways
you can set up this.

00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:53.550
The suggestion is, for sure,
do route-based to start

00:35:53.550 --> 00:35:55.740
with because it's more flexible.

00:35:55.740 --> 00:35:58.290
But the thing I always
suggest to customer

00:35:58.290 --> 00:36:02.550
is to start, if possible,
with the Cloud Router and BGP.

00:36:02.550 --> 00:36:03.450
Why?

00:36:03.450 --> 00:36:06.860
Because the moment you switch
to Dedicated Interconnect,

00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:10.020
the routing will be
exactly the same.

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:13.160
So you can test
and get confident

00:36:13.160 --> 00:36:16.280
of your routing
setup, VPN, and then

00:36:16.280 --> 00:36:18.080
move to the dedicated
interconnect,

00:36:18.080 --> 00:36:20.670
and you don't change a thing.

00:36:20.670 --> 00:36:23.610
You can actually
use VPN as a backup

00:36:23.610 --> 00:36:24.990
of Dedicated Interconnect.

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:29.250
And this is what I'm showing
you in the last slide.

00:36:29.250 --> 00:36:35.670
So what I'm leveraging here
is the global routing feature

00:36:35.670 --> 00:36:37.210
of the Cloud Router.

00:36:37.210 --> 00:36:41.930
So let's say that I want
to give reachability

00:36:41.930 --> 00:36:43.800
to us-east subnets.

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:47.070
I send two advertisements
from the us-east Cloud

00:36:47.070 --> 00:36:51.810
Router on the green
region with met-120,

00:36:51.810 --> 00:36:54.090
so met is a BGP attributes,
basically, that you

00:36:54.090 --> 00:36:56.370
can set on the Cloud Router.

00:36:56.370 --> 00:37:02.070
And also, the Cloud
Router in us-west

00:37:02.070 --> 00:37:05.550
knows about the us-east1 subnet.

00:37:05.550 --> 00:37:09.840
But we automatically
add an inter-region cost

00:37:09.840 --> 00:37:13.050
to that route, which
is that 201, which

00:37:13.050 --> 00:37:16.800
means that when Cloud
Router in the orange region

00:37:16.800 --> 00:37:22.020
advertises subnetworks, they
automatically get an additional

00:37:22.020 --> 00:37:27.440
met and higher met than
120, meaning 120 plus 201.

00:37:27.440 --> 00:37:29.280
And that's 201 is
just an example.

00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:32.070
It's dynamically
calculated by the platform.

00:37:32.070 --> 00:37:36.300
And this way, you're sure
that both R1 and R2 use

00:37:36.300 --> 00:37:39.600
the Interconnect
default. And when

00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:44.050
those Interconnects are down,
you use a VPN as a backup.

00:37:44.050 --> 00:37:46.830
The same thing you can
do on the reverse side.

00:37:46.830 --> 00:37:49.230
And on the reverse side,
it's actually easier

00:37:49.230 --> 00:37:53.030
because even if you advertise
the same met from on-prem

00:37:53.030 --> 00:37:56.190
to your Cloud Routers,
we automatically

00:37:56.190 --> 00:38:01.125
add that inter-region cost
when the Cloud Routers that

00:38:01.125 --> 00:38:05.140
lives in the orange region
program the routes on the VPC.

00:38:05.140 --> 00:38:07.670
We automatically do
that because the subnets

00:38:07.670 --> 00:38:09.870
are living in a different
region, in the green one.

00:38:09.870 --> 00:38:13.680
So that gives you automatic
load balancing and usage

00:38:13.680 --> 00:38:16.920
of the Dedicated Interconnect
as a primary and all leading VPN

00:38:16.920 --> 00:38:19.690
only as a backup.

00:38:19.690 --> 00:38:22.620
And with that, we
go to Kamal, who

00:38:22.620 --> 00:38:26.580
will tell you how PayPal is
using VPC in their production

00:38:26.580 --> 00:38:27.080
network.

00:38:27.080 --> 00:38:27.878
Thank you.

00:38:27.878 --> 00:38:31.364
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:34.167 --> 00:38:36.500
KAMAL CONGEVARAM MURALIDHARAN:
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:38:36.500 --> 00:38:37.720
My name is Kamal.

00:38:37.720 --> 00:38:39.760
I'm part of the core
platform and infrastructure

00:38:39.760 --> 00:38:41.810
team at PayPal.

00:38:41.810 --> 00:38:45.490
So at PayPal, we started
our public cloud journey

00:38:45.490 --> 00:38:47.350
a couple of years back.

00:38:47.350 --> 00:38:51.010
So when we started
looking at how PayPal

00:38:51.010 --> 00:38:53.590
can expand into
public cloud, and we

00:38:53.590 --> 00:38:56.360
are having one of the
largest private data centers.

00:38:56.360 --> 00:38:58.600
So we started looking
at what is needed

00:38:58.600 --> 00:39:01.540
and what we need to do
to get into public cloud.

00:39:01.540 --> 00:39:04.490
We soon realized two
important things.

00:39:04.490 --> 00:39:08.770
One is that it is not a
one-year or a two-year journey

00:39:08.770 --> 00:39:11.510
and just like that we can
just jump into a public cloud.

00:39:11.510 --> 00:39:14.610
It is a multi-year journey
because it involves

00:39:14.610 --> 00:39:17.410
a culture change, mindset
change, toolset change,

00:39:17.410 --> 00:39:21.230
and even some application
rewrite as we move into that.

00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:23.410
The second thing we
realized was none

00:39:23.410 --> 00:39:26.050
of the public cloud
had all the features

00:39:26.050 --> 00:39:29.410
for supporting enterprises
to run in a more

00:39:29.410 --> 00:39:31.900
secure and a scalable way.

00:39:31.900 --> 00:39:35.470
So it means that we need a
partner to journey with us,

00:39:35.470 --> 00:39:39.010
and we travel along with them
in this multi-year journey

00:39:39.010 --> 00:39:43.270
to get to the state where
we can run a big enterprise

00:39:43.270 --> 00:39:45.170
on public cloud.

00:39:45.170 --> 00:39:49.220
So with that, one of the key
things we started looking at is

00:39:49.220 --> 00:39:53.620
that how do you build building
blocks in the public cloud

00:39:53.620 --> 00:39:56.920
so that we can start figuring
out how to move things

00:39:56.920 --> 00:39:59.780
in a phased manner because
we cannot move everything.

00:39:59.780 --> 00:40:02.770
So one of the key pieces
which we started looking at

00:40:02.770 --> 00:40:07.150
is, how do you build
this VPC in public cloud?

00:40:07.150 --> 00:40:09.300
So with that, I'll go
to one of the use cases

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:12.600
which we started with and how
we went through the process

00:40:12.600 --> 00:40:16.440
of moving into public cloud.

00:40:16.440 --> 00:40:20.017
So the first use case we
took was a dev use case,

00:40:20.017 --> 00:40:20.850
which is basically--

00:40:20.850 --> 00:40:23.310
PayPal has thousands
of developers,

00:40:23.310 --> 00:40:27.270
and each of those
developers needs their space

00:40:27.270 --> 00:40:30.840
to go and do creative things,
like creating their VMs,

00:40:30.840 --> 00:40:33.030
deploying their services,
running obligations,

00:40:33.030 --> 00:40:35.310
or do things, which
is something we want

00:40:35.310 --> 00:40:37.270
to provide the space for them.

00:40:37.270 --> 00:40:39.960
So when you look at
that, what they need

00:40:39.960 --> 00:40:43.710
is autonomy in doing that.

00:40:43.710 --> 00:40:48.080
I would say that DevOps is
a very widely used term,

00:40:48.080 --> 00:40:51.420
but the meaning of DevOps
varies from company

00:40:51.420 --> 00:40:53.610
to company, person to person.

00:40:53.610 --> 00:40:56.340
But what we really think
is that give the autonomy

00:40:56.340 --> 00:41:00.490
to the developer so that they
can do what they need to do,

00:41:00.490 --> 00:41:02.520
and we don't step
into their thing.

00:41:02.520 --> 00:41:04.984
At the same time, there
are a lot of other things

00:41:04.984 --> 00:41:06.900
which developers doesn't
want to take care of.

00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:10.420
For example, when we
started a Google Cloud,

00:41:10.420 --> 00:41:13.120
there was nothing
called a Shared VPC.

00:41:13.120 --> 00:41:17.250
So the model which existed
was, you create a project.

00:41:17.250 --> 00:41:18.870
And inside the
project, there will

00:41:18.870 --> 00:41:22.140
be a network, which means
that every project has

00:41:22.140 --> 00:41:25.920
its own network, which means
that every developer-- if I

00:41:25.920 --> 00:41:28.140
have to give a
project, then we also

00:41:28.140 --> 00:41:31.050
have to create a network
inside the project, which

00:41:31.050 --> 00:41:33.300
means that we need to
allocate an IP range.

00:41:33.300 --> 00:41:36.930
So we are talking about
hundreds of projects-- even more

00:41:36.930 --> 00:41:38.440
than that, thousands
of projects.

00:41:38.440 --> 00:41:40.920
And it's a big nightmare
for us to manage IP ranges

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:42.540
for each of these projects.

00:41:42.540 --> 00:41:44.850
The second thing is that--

00:41:44.850 --> 00:41:47.760
another main principle we
talk about for public cloud

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:49.590
is, everything
should be automated,

00:41:49.590 --> 00:41:52.210
which means that when
we set up something,

00:41:52.210 --> 00:41:55.920
it's not just the automation
to create the initial project,

00:41:55.920 --> 00:41:58.840
but any maintenance after
that should be automated.

00:41:58.840 --> 00:42:01.440
So if we are creating a
project for a developer

00:42:01.440 --> 00:42:04.920
and if the network is within
that-- so any interconnectivity

00:42:04.920 --> 00:42:07.440
configuration or a
gateway configuration

00:42:07.440 --> 00:42:08.980
all exist within the network.

00:42:08.980 --> 00:42:10.650
So if you have to
make a change, it's

00:42:10.650 --> 00:42:12.690
not making change
in one project.

00:42:12.690 --> 00:42:14.970
We are talking about
going and making changes

00:42:14.970 --> 00:42:16.330
in thousands of projects.

00:42:16.330 --> 00:42:21.160
So it created a pretty difficult
for us to scale in that model.

00:42:21.160 --> 00:42:23.550
And that's where
we work with Google

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:25.900
and then we partnered up
and say that, hey, these

00:42:25.900 --> 00:42:26.740
are the problems.

00:42:26.740 --> 00:42:29.020
And that's where we
started looking at--

00:42:29.020 --> 00:42:31.950
Google introduced this
concept of shared VPC.

00:42:31.950 --> 00:42:34.726
So I think Neha and
Emanuele explained

00:42:34.726 --> 00:42:36.600
all the topologies and
everything, so I'm not

00:42:36.600 --> 00:42:37.641
putting any diagram here.

00:42:37.641 --> 00:42:40.470
But I'm going to explain
what problems we faced

00:42:40.470 --> 00:42:44.440
when we started initially and
how we saw it in the shared VPC

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:46.130
model.

00:42:46.130 --> 00:42:50.130
So as I said, in the
current industry,

00:42:50.130 --> 00:42:52.300
there are two
schools of thought.

00:42:52.300 --> 00:42:55.200
One is a complete
DevOps model, where

00:42:55.200 --> 00:42:59.470
you give complete control to
the developer from start to end.

00:42:59.470 --> 00:43:01.020
That means that you
create an account

00:43:01.020 --> 00:43:02.561
and give everything
to the developer,

00:43:02.561 --> 00:43:05.910
and developer manages from
network to compute or deploying

00:43:05.910 --> 00:43:07.560
applications and everything.

00:43:07.560 --> 00:43:09.960
And there's another extreme
thought, which is basically

00:43:09.960 --> 00:43:13.140
completely anti-DevOps,
where everything

00:43:13.140 --> 00:43:16.590
is done by somebody else,
and developer gives support.

00:43:16.590 --> 00:43:20.980
So at PayPal, what we did is
that we took a hybrid approach.

00:43:20.980 --> 00:43:25.410
We really figured out what each
team should be responsible for

00:43:25.410 --> 00:43:27.120
and where we should
draw the line

00:43:27.120 --> 00:43:29.310
and say that, these
are all your developer

00:43:29.310 --> 00:43:31.440
responsibilities,
like compute, storage,

00:43:31.440 --> 00:43:32.880
your code, and everything.

00:43:32.880 --> 00:43:33.730
You manage it.

00:43:33.730 --> 00:43:36.280
But anything below, we want
to really standardize it.

00:43:36.280 --> 00:43:38.166
And especially in
a big enterprise,

00:43:38.166 --> 00:43:40.290
when you have thousands of
developers and thousands

00:43:40.290 --> 00:43:42.300
of projects,
standardization becomes

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:46.500
very critical in terms of
security and also auditing.

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:48.037
If you want to
change something, you

00:43:48.037 --> 00:43:49.370
need to know where to change it.

00:43:49.370 --> 00:43:52.120
If it is in many places,
it becomes very tricky.

00:43:52.120 --> 00:43:54.830
So if you look at it,
as I said, IP management

00:43:54.830 --> 00:43:56.040
was one of the problems.

00:43:56.040 --> 00:43:58.410
Allocating IP ranges,
assigning them,

00:43:58.410 --> 00:44:00.960
and when the project is deleted,
re-assigning it somewhere--

00:44:00.960 --> 00:44:02.490
it was becoming a challenge.

00:44:02.490 --> 00:44:05.250
With Shared VPC, the
model we are after

00:44:05.250 --> 00:44:09.210
was that we have many
security zones in PayPal.

00:44:09.210 --> 00:44:11.070
Each security zone
is carved out based

00:44:11.070 --> 00:44:14.010
on what data can be
stored, what data

00:44:14.010 --> 00:44:17.100
can be accessed, what
services can exist,

00:44:17.100 --> 00:44:18.630
how the services can interact.

00:44:18.630 --> 00:44:21.660
There's many criterias based
on which we carve out zones.

00:44:21.660 --> 00:44:24.160
For example, there may be a
zone where the data resides,

00:44:24.160 --> 00:44:26.160
which means that nobody
can enter into the zone

00:44:26.160 --> 00:44:28.140
except through a single
channel, and that

00:44:28.140 --> 00:44:30.280
would be protected
with firewalls,

00:44:30.280 --> 00:44:32.200
data loss, everything.

00:44:32.200 --> 00:44:36.450
So we have this concept already
built into our private cloud.

00:44:36.450 --> 00:44:38.880
We want to bring a similar
concept into public cloud.

00:44:38.880 --> 00:44:43.130
That's where this model
of VPC became very useful,

00:44:43.130 --> 00:44:46.140
where we said that
every VPC we create

00:44:46.140 --> 00:44:48.150
is going to be a
security zone, which

00:44:48.150 --> 00:44:51.030
means that, within that
security zone, it's open,

00:44:51.030 --> 00:44:53.210
that if there are two
VMs, they can communicate.

00:44:53.210 --> 00:44:55.770
But any query to come
into that security zone--

00:44:55.770 --> 00:44:57.660
it has to go through
a firewall or it'll

00:44:57.660 --> 00:45:03.030
have a VPC peering with more
security and scrutinization.

00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:06.960
The second thing is that, as I
said, once we have Shared VPC,

00:45:06.960 --> 00:45:09.600
the management becomes more
simpler because we just

00:45:09.600 --> 00:45:13.080
need to manage the connectivity
at the Shared VPC level.

00:45:13.080 --> 00:45:15.099
Every service project
we just created

00:45:15.099 --> 00:45:16.390
doesn't need to worry about it.

00:45:16.390 --> 00:45:18.210
So as far as the
developers are concerned,

00:45:18.210 --> 00:45:20.580
they just keep creating
VM, storage, and everything

00:45:20.580 --> 00:45:24.300
we started with a
VPN based approach,

00:45:24.300 --> 00:45:27.180
and then now we are moving
to a data Interconnect, which

00:45:27.180 --> 00:45:29.580
means that there's no impact
on the developers of us

00:45:29.580 --> 00:45:32.151
because everything is centrally
managed at the Shared VPC

00:45:32.151 --> 00:45:33.360
level.

00:45:33.360 --> 00:45:36.390
So this is a functional
representation

00:45:36.390 --> 00:45:39.780
of how we divided
the functionalities

00:45:39.780 --> 00:45:43.920
and felt that this is the
optimal way so that we

00:45:43.920 --> 00:45:47.010
give enough freedom for
developers to play around

00:45:47.010 --> 00:45:49.440
with public cloud, do
things what they need.

00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:51.390
But at the same
time, we don't need

00:45:51.390 --> 00:45:54.100
to overburden them with
responsibilities which they

00:45:54.100 --> 00:45:55.470
don't need to take care of.

00:45:55.470 --> 00:45:58.950
So if you look at it, we
have a Enterprise Cloud team

00:45:58.950 --> 00:46:02.460
which comes with best
practices of how many VPCs

00:46:02.460 --> 00:46:05.880
has to be created, how the
VPCs has to be interconnected,

00:46:05.880 --> 00:46:08.550
like peered or [? peered, ?]
how they have to connect back

00:46:08.550 --> 00:46:11.040
to the on-prem, where should
be the routing configured.

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:14.010
All these are
centralized and automated

00:46:14.010 --> 00:46:17.530
through one single mechanism
so we manage everything down.

00:46:17.530 --> 00:46:19.320
And at the same time,
we have automation

00:46:19.320 --> 00:46:22.980
to create projects which
sets a project up, puts

00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:25.500
the right developers there,
and grants permission

00:46:25.500 --> 00:46:26.341
to this network.

00:46:26.341 --> 00:46:26.840
That's it.

00:46:26.840 --> 00:46:29.400
Once they get the project,
within the project,

00:46:29.400 --> 00:46:31.240
they have an autonomy
to do anything.

00:46:31.240 --> 00:46:34.330
So in this mechanism,
we were able to scale it

00:46:34.330 --> 00:46:36.360
to thousands of
projects, and we are

00:46:36.360 --> 00:46:39.390
able to manage it without any
impact on the future changes

00:46:39.390 --> 00:46:41.692
because we all know that
things [? airwall. ?]

00:46:41.692 --> 00:46:43.650
The cloud is going to
[? airwall. ?] That means

00:46:43.650 --> 00:46:45.310
we should also move
along with that.

00:46:45.310 --> 00:46:47.790
So we build the
foundation such a way

00:46:47.790 --> 00:46:53.679
that we can make more changes
in the future with less impact.

00:46:53.679 --> 00:46:55.220
Also, another
principle we talk about

00:46:55.220 --> 00:46:57.120
is there's another
use case, which

00:46:57.120 --> 00:46:59.450
is a test zone, which is
basically running PayPal.com

00:46:59.450 --> 00:47:03.600
inside PayPal, which means that
it is a complete replication

00:47:03.600 --> 00:47:04.910
of PayPal inside PayPal.

00:47:04.910 --> 00:47:07.680
That means that it
is used for our test

00:47:07.680 --> 00:47:10.440
environments, the integration
testings, and everything.

00:47:10.440 --> 00:47:13.980
Now, when we are up at a model,
which I showed previously,

00:47:13.980 --> 00:47:16.470
our goal is to see
that the same model can

00:47:16.470 --> 00:47:19.800
be applied for any use cases
which comes later, like test.

00:47:19.800 --> 00:47:22.750
Tomorrow it could be production
or later, something else.

00:47:22.750 --> 00:47:25.690
So in this case, if you
look at the use case,

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:27.750
it's something a lesser scale.

00:47:27.750 --> 00:47:29.430
In terms of number
of projects, we

00:47:29.430 --> 00:47:32.610
are talking about only
a few tens of projects.

00:47:32.610 --> 00:47:34.200
But it's a huge resource.

00:47:34.200 --> 00:47:36.660
It's like thousands
of VMs, thousands

00:47:36.660 --> 00:47:38.910
of gigabytes of
storage and everything

00:47:38.910 --> 00:47:40.990
because it's complete
PayPal inside.

00:47:40.990 --> 00:47:43.590
But if you go into the
model, it's the same.

00:47:43.590 --> 00:47:44.880
We didn't change anything.

00:47:44.880 --> 00:47:47.580
We created a VPC, which
was already automated,

00:47:47.580 --> 00:47:48.950
which has all the features.

00:47:48.950 --> 00:47:51.090
We created the project
in the same way.

00:47:51.090 --> 00:47:53.799
We just need to assign different
themes to these projects.

00:47:53.799 --> 00:47:56.340
And within that, they were able
to do everything they wanted.

00:47:56.340 --> 00:48:00.150
So the model is scalable to any
use case which you bring it.

00:48:00.150 --> 00:48:02.910
And we are in the process
of, next year, trying

00:48:02.910 --> 00:48:06.420
to get public cloud production
use cases into public cloud.

00:48:06.420 --> 00:48:08.160
So we don't need to change much.

00:48:08.160 --> 00:48:10.020
The same automation
which we created

00:48:10.020 --> 00:48:11.640
is going to help
us do this again.

00:48:14.300 --> 00:48:16.880
As I said, its a
multi-year journey.

00:48:16.880 --> 00:48:21.800
We all realize that not
everything is available today,

00:48:21.800 --> 00:48:24.320
and we have to get this going.

00:48:24.320 --> 00:48:27.840
So we have seen
that VPC is common,

00:48:27.840 --> 00:48:30.770
but, again, not every
services are routed VPC.

00:48:30.770 --> 00:48:33.140
For example, we wanted
to create database

00:48:33.140 --> 00:48:36.050
with the private IP assigned
from the VPC range, which

00:48:36.050 --> 00:48:37.100
will become available.

00:48:37.100 --> 00:48:38.420
We'll start adopting it.

00:48:38.420 --> 00:48:40.820
IP alias is a great
feature, which was released.

00:48:40.820 --> 00:48:44.600
For us, we have a container
networking technology where

00:48:44.600 --> 00:48:46.170
we assign IP to container.

00:48:46.170 --> 00:48:48.290
So for that, IP Alias
is a great thing.

00:48:48.290 --> 00:48:50.360
So we are going
to start using it.

00:48:50.360 --> 00:48:53.660
And similarly, as this
technology evolves,

00:48:53.660 --> 00:48:54.890
our tools also evolve.

00:48:54.890 --> 00:48:58.280
For example, we had events
which have been consumed.

00:48:58.280 --> 00:49:01.640
Now, since we have this host
in a service project isolation,

00:49:01.640 --> 00:49:03.410
we need to figure out
how to collect even

00:49:03.410 --> 00:49:05.660
from different service
projects as from a single place

00:49:05.660 --> 00:49:08.150
because we don't want to deploy
something on every service

00:49:08.150 --> 00:49:08.680
project.

00:49:08.680 --> 00:49:11.570
So we are also evolving as
that technology evolves.

00:49:11.570 --> 00:49:14.330
And as Google is maturing
enough more with this,

00:49:14.330 --> 00:49:17.080
we will also be moving
along with that.

00:49:17.080 --> 00:49:18.210
Thank you.

00:49:18.210 --> 00:49:18.810
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:18.810 --> 00:49:22.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

